<p>Taiwan President Lai Ching-te said Sunday that Taiwan “will never be sacrificed or traded away” and described U.S. arms sales as a security commitment grounded in law, in his first direct response to President Trump’s remarks after a summit with Chinese President Xi Jinping in Beijing. Lai said Taiwan would not provoke conflict with China, but would not give up its sovereignty, dignity, democratic system or “free way of life” under pressure. President Trump said after the Beijing summit that he had not approved a pending roughly $14 billion arms sale to Taiwan, saying he “may do it” or “may not do it,” that the decision would depend on China and describing the sale as a negotiating chip. He also warned Taiwan against declaring formal independence and said he made no commitments to Xi on Taiwan. U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer said Sunday there was “no change” in U.S. policy on Taiwan and that Trump was weighing how to approach the arms decision.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/3_nCR_n91CaxsMQHsS2t32H62ymJMyhNhzf3b7ET85oQrknOZDYhTC6hjeF2Whnxl1QxY0F7v-xrqyvmW9ruqWaATJiXsbdK_NfErGNN-pbdVlikKIaJjP1U_qcLBrVGuVZXHp_vFqbc8pXzyEV2dEes7sc1FZOjg1cwIN5EVSyYNThClOoV61l0qK32dsM6KOc4x4a69S9Z5Syc5j3Sm4QVHAcl7NYVpIz4yPW5r2Kwk9e8AJcM_kDwwUsSB0Ck7PbGs1k0GtB8MB8lbg' alt='Taiwan President Lai Ching-te speaks during a press conference in Taipei on Feb. 3, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Five-point response: Lai presented his remarks as a five-point Facebook statement after hearing a national security team report.</li><li>Taipei-Washington contacts: Taiwan’s Deputy Foreign Minister Chen Ming-chi said the government remains in constant contact with Washington over weapons support.</li><li>Presidential office framing: Spokesperson Karen Kuo said China’s heightened military threats were the factor undermining Indo-Pacific and Taiwan Strait stability.</li></ul><blockquote>I haven’t approved it yet. We’re going to see what happens - President Donald Trump</blockq